Part B: m=0 special case -- confirm EXPONENTIAL (not power-law) convergence
====================================================================================================
A_0(gamma)=0 predicts term_0(n,gamma)-1/gamma decays FASTER than any
power of 1/n. Cited exact formula: term_0=(1-(1-gamma)^(n+1))/gamma,
so term_0-1/gamma = -(1-gamma)^(n+1)/gamma -- literally exponential.
Confirmed directly (both routes, Beta-integral quadrature and cited
closed form, agree):
  gamma=0.3 n=100: quad diff=-7.547111856e-16, closed-form diff=-7.547111856e-16, agree to 4.20202e-82
  gamma=0.3 n=1000: quad diff=0.0, closed-form diff=-2.924265493e-155, agree to 2.92427e-155
  gamma=0.5 n=100: quad diff=-7.888609052e-31, closed-form diff=-7.888609052e-31, agree to 0.0
  gamma=0.5 n=1000: quad diff=-2.108439589e-81, closed-form diff=-9.332636185e-302, agree to 2.10844e-81
  gamma=0.8 n=100: quad diff=-3.169126501e-71, closed-form diff=-3.169126501e-71, agree to 5.2177e-82
  gamma=0.8 n=1000: quad diff=0.0, closed-form diff=-2.678771518e-700, agree to 2.67877e-700

